{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The goal is to learn quickly whether an idea is worth pursuing, and the code will be thrown away, so reading it adds little. Keep it away from production data, real credentials, and shared infrastructure, and don't let it quietly turn into the MVP."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Demos mostly need to work reliably on the happy path, but people in the room will form judgments from them, so skim the parts that touch data, auth, or external services and test the demo flow end to end. Make sure the demo doesn't show capabilities that are faked or fragile without saying so."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, security exposure, and code you'll probably keep building on. Let AI write most of it, but read every line of security-sensitive code (auth, payments, data access, input handling, secrets), review the architecture and data model, and back the rest with automated tests."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "A team has to maintain, debug, and extend this code for years, so every change needs someone who understands it and owns it, just as with human-written code. AI can still do most of the typing; standard code review keeps the codebase understandable and catches subtle bugs that tests miss."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, every line needs a clear human author who can justify it to auditors and fix it under pressure, and subtle defects can cause serious harm. Use AI for suggestions, analysis, test generation, and review assistance, but have engineers write or fully rewrite shipped code under your existing verification and certification processes."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Read AI-written code more closely as the cost of a defect, the lifespan of the code, and the number of people who depend on it grow: anything goes for throwaway prototypes, careful review in production, and human-authored code in critical systems. The level is a minimum for each stage, not a ceiling. Security-sensitive code (auth, payments, personal data, secrets) should get line-by-line review from the MVP stage onward whatever the general level, and when prototype code moves into a later stage, review it to that stage's standard instead of carrying it over unread."
}

